<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:views="using:Lattice.App.Views">
  <!-- Marker classes for the one alignment invariant that spans the whole app (issues #180, #185):
       wherever a FIXED-SIZE MARK is centred beside a text label, the mark must sit on the label's
       reference BAND, not on its line box.

       WHY THE MARKUP CANNOT JUST CENTRE BOTH. VerticalAlignment="Center" centres each child's
       LAYOUT BOX, and a TextBlock's box is a line box (ascent + descent + line gap) whose glyph ink
       sits at a PER-FONT offset inside it. "Centre both boxes" therefore aligns in one font and
       misaligns in the next. Measured at the sites these classes serve, mark-box centre to band
       centre: 1.241 px off in Helvetica at the Event Log's priority pills, 1.063 px at the data
       cells and the command-bar freshness caption, against 0.024 px in Times New Roman. The defect
       is that whole SPREAD — over a pixel of drift with nothing but the font changing — and
       Helvetica is both the worst of it and what the app actually renders with on macOS, since
       Lattice pins no font and Avalonia's Skia font manager resolves the platform default to it
       (#179).

       These classes collapse the label's layout box onto a fixed reference band, so centring the
       pair centres the mark on that band by construction, in any font. Which band is the site's
       business — see the two classes below.

       THEY LIVE HERE, NOT IN DataGridStyles.axaml. They were born there with the four data cells
       #184 fixed, but the invariant was never grid-scoped: #185's sites are command bars, a
       button, and the Statistics legend chips. Rather than pull chrome into the grid's style sheet
       the classes moved to this shared surface — single-copy, as they always were.

       EVERY CONSUMER OWES ONE THING BACK: UseLayoutRounding="False" on the panel that holds the
       pair. The collapse margin is fractional by nature, and snapping an arrange rect back to whole
       pixels is the other half of the same defect. It must be off for the MARK too, which carries
       no class of its own and inherits the panel's value. A site that applies a class without it is
       caught by the rendered layer of StatusCellAlignmentTests / ChromeAlignmentTests.

       VerticalAlignment=Center is likewise load-bearing, not decoration, and is why these classes
       set it rather than leaving it to the call site: a StackPanel child defaults to STRETCH, so
       the label's box would be stretched back to the panel's height with the text drawn at its
       top, and collapsing a box that is then stretched again changes nothing (measured: the margin
       applied and the mark did not move by a thousandth). The band only becomes the box when the
       box is centred. -->

  <!-- WORDS. The band is the one the label's own script is read against: the cap band for Latin,
       the ideograph em band for the zh-CN UI, whose strings are Han and do not share the Latin line
       box at all (see TextInkCollapseConverter.WordBandFor). The band is a fixed reference glyph,
       NOT the live word: labels differ in descender content ("Suspended" has one, "Running" does
       not; the freshness caption rewrites itself on every poll), so a band read off the live text
       would re-centre the pair every time the text changed — measured under that mutation, 1.24 px
       of shift. That is the reason the snooze pill's digit-band remedy (#176/#179) could not simply
       be copied here.

       WHY CAP HEIGHT AND NOT X-HEIGHT, for Latin. These labels are sentence case ("Running",
       "Computing", "Updated 4 m ago"), so the tallest thing on the line at the mark's shoulder is a
       capital; centring the mark on the cap band is the rule Fluent/Material/Carbon all state for
       icon-beside-label. An x-height band would seat the mark ~0.6 px lower and let its box overhang
       the ascenders on both sides, which reads as a sunk icon next to a capital. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.wordAligned">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin">
      <MultiBinding Converter="{x:Static views:TextInkCollapseConverter.Words}">
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontFamily" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontSize" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontWeight" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontStyle" />
      </MultiBinding>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!-- FIGURES. The same mechanism for a label that is digits, not words: Tasks' Deadline, whose
       "MM-dd HH:mm" is formatted with the invariant culture and so never contains a letter in any
       locale. The band is therefore the digit repertoire — the one the snooze pill uses — and not
       the cap band (Codex P2 on PR #184): a face with old-style figures (Georgia) draws digits that
       descend below the baseline and are shorter than its capitals, so aligning a warning glyph to
       "H" there would centre it on ink the site never shows. Everything else about the class is
       identical, the panel's UseLayoutRounding="False" included. -->
  <Style Selector="TextBlock.digitAligned">
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="Margin">
      <MultiBinding Converter="{x:Static views:TextInkCollapseConverter.Instance}">
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontFamily" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontSize" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontWeight" />
        <Binding RelativeSource="{RelativeSource Self}" Path="FontStyle" />
      </MultiBinding>
    </Setter>
  </Style>
</Styles>
